  It's a sound business decision.  Make a good game not based on
eye-candy and people will just keep playing it.  And stop buying 
new games.  

  Making a good game would also "wreck the curve".  ID gave away
the first episode of Doom for free.  I happily paid for the full 
game because I knew I'd like it.  I had shelves full of bad games
purchased based on magazine reviews.  (Note: Computer Gaming 
World never reviewed a bad game during their first ten years of
existence.)  Quickly all games had to have free demos, or people
wouldn't buy them.  And people still wouldn't buy the bad games.


  Computer magazines got the rug pulled from under them.  Lying 
about a game with a free demo was playing Russian Roulette, since
not everyone had access to the Internet.  Then PC Gamer started
up and came with a disk of game demos. (First floppy, then CD's,
with the occasional DVD.)  Lying about a game in a magazine that
also gave the reader a demo of the game is a really bad idea.


  And no Doctorate of Business Administration wants smart, 
